    "prompt": "Function `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` in src/click/_winconsole.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\ndef readinto(self, b):\n        bytes_to_be_read = len(b)\n        if not bytes_to_be_read:\n            return 0\n        elif bytes_to_be_read % 2:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"cannot read odd number of bytes from UTF-16-LE encoded console\"\n            )\n\n        buffer = get_buffer(b, writable=True)\n        code_units_to_be_read = bytes_to_be_read // 2\n        code_units_read = c_ulong()\n\n        rv = ReadConsoleW(\n            HANDLE(self.handle),\n            buffer,\n            code_units_to_be_read,\n            byref(code_units_read),\n            None,\n        )\n        if GetLastError() == ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED:\n            # wait for KeyboardInterrupt\n            time.sleep(0.1)\n        if not rv:\n            raise OSError(f\"Windows error: {GetLastError()}\")\n\n        if buffer[0] == EOF:\n            return 0\n        return 2 * code_units_read.value\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: readinto(self, b)\ncalls: GetLastError, HANDLE, OSError, ReadConsoleW, ValueError, byref, c_ulong, get_buffer, len, time.sleep\nreturn statements: return 0 | return 0 | return 2 * code_units_read.value\nraise statements: raise ValueError(\n                \"cannot read odd number of bytes from UTF-16-L | raise OSError(f\"Windows error: {GetLastError()}\")\ncontrol flow: if=4 loops=0 try=0 with=0 returns=3 yields=0 awaits=0\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. The `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` function returns the number of bytes actually read into the buffer.\nS2. If an odd number of bytes is requested, the function raises a `ValueError` because UTF-16-LE encoding requires reading an even number of bytes.\nS3. The `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` function exists to handle the intricacies of reading from a Windows console, particularly when dealing with UTF-16-LE encoded text.\nS4. If the read operation fails, it raises an `OSError` with the Windows error code.\nS5. The function uses the `ReadConsoleW` function from the Windows API to perform the actual read operation.\nS6. The function relies on the `get_buffer` helper function to obtain a writable buffer view.\nS7. If the read operation is interrupted, the function waits briefly for a `KeyboardInterrupt`.\nS8. The `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` function is designed to handle the intricacies of reading from a Windows console, particularly when dealing with UTF-16-LE encoded text.\nS9. If the first byte in the buffer is an EOF marker, the function returns 0, indicating no data was read.\nS10. The function has no side effects other than modifying the provided buffer and potentially raising exceptions.\nS11. The `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` function takes a single parameter `b`, which is a buffer object that must be writable.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supported\nS2: contradicted -- <evidence, at most 12 words>\nS3: unresolved",
